# Article: Toolset (dotnet)

Quality toolset for C# / .NET projects.

These are the project's quality tools. Where a role prompt names a
purpose (coverage, mutation, crap, dry, boundaries, acceptance), use
exactly the command below — no substitutes.

## acceptance

    dotnet test <acceptance-project>

Reqnroll for Gherkin acceptance. Do NOT use SpecFlow (discontinued 2024); do not let an agent add it.

## boundaries

    dotnet test <arch-tests-project>

Architecture rules as NetArchTest.Rules (or ArchUnitNET) tests in a dedicated test project; failures are blocking.

## coverage

    dotnet test --collect:"XPlat Code Coverage;Format=lcov"

coverlet writes lcov to <tests>/TestResults/<run-id>/coverage.info. Regenerate before reading any coverage-derived metric; stale coverage invalidates everything built on it.

## crap

    crap4net --lcov <coverage.info> <source-dir>

CRAP = complexity^2 x (1-coverage)^3 + complexity, computed per method from source + lcov. Bar: every method <= 6 (exit 2 lists offenders). Requires fresh coverage first.

## dry

    jscpd --min-tokens 50 --reporters console <source-dir>

Duplication within the slice's scope gets removed or explicitly justified.

## mutation

    dotnet stryker

Run from the test project directory. Surviving mutants in the slice's scope are work: kill them with better tests or record precisely why each survivor is unkillable.

## test

    dotnet test

Full unit suite; green before any handoff. Test stack: xUnit as runner, Shouldly for assertions (value.ShouldBe(expected), Should.Throw<T>), NSubstitute for test doubles. Do not use bare Assert.* or other mocking libraries in new tests.
